---
import Layout from '../components/Layout.astro';
import { GradeBadge } from '../components/ui';
import { GRADES, GRADE_ORDER } from '../lib/grades';
import { FLAG_LABELS, FLAG_DEFINITIONS } from '../lib/flags';
import type { Flag } from '../lib/flags';

const flags = Object.keys(FLAG_LABELS) as Flag[];
---

<Layout
  title="Methodology"
  description="The full grading rubric behind Evidence Stack: how evidence grades and effect sizes are assigned, what the flags mean, who reviews, how corrections are handled, and how the site is funded."
>
  <article>
    <div class="page-head">
      <h1>Methodology</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Everything on this site follows from two rules: grades are per claim, never per
        compound, and the species behind the evidence is never hidden. This page is the full
        contract — how grades are assigned, what can move them, and who is accountable.
      </p>
    </div>

    <section>
      <h2>The evidence grade — how much do we know?</h2>
      <p>
        Each claim (a compound → outcome pair, like "creatine → muscle mass") carries one
        letter grade. The grade measures the <i>state of knowledge</i>, not how good the
        compound is:
      </p>
      <div class="grade-list">
        {GRADE_ORDER.map((g) => (
          <div class="grade-row">
            <GradeBadge grade={g} size={32} />
            <div class="grade-text">{GRADES[g]}</div>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
      <p>
        A grade never collapses across claims. A compound with an A for one outcome and a D
        for another is shown exactly that way, because that <i>is</i> the state of knowledge.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Effect size — if it works, how much?</h2>
      <p>
        Shown separately as <b>large / moderate / small / negligible / unclear</b>, and never
        merged into the grade. A compound can be grade A with a negligible effect — rigorously
        proven to barely matter — and that combination is one of the most useful things this
        register can tell you. Where trials report standardised effect sizes we use them;
        where they don't, the call is the reviewer's and the studies to check it against are
        listed on the claim.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Flags</h2>
      <p>Small, factual annotations shown on both claims and individual studies:</p>
      <dl>
        {flags.map((f) => (
          <>
            <dt>{FLAG_LABELS[f]}</dt>
            <dd>{FLAG_DEFINITIONS[f]}</dd>
          </>
        ))}
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What we record per study</h2>
      <p>
        Species · design · sample size · duration · dose and form · outcome measured · funding
        source · trial registry ID · link to the primary source · the reviewer's note on what
        this study contributes to the claim. Every one of these is displayed, not summarised
        away. Mouse results and human results never share a visual treatment.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Grade movement</h2>
      <p>
        Every claim's grade is dated and carries its history. When new evidence moves a grade,
        that change is a first-class event: it appears on the compound's timeline, on the home
        page, and in the newsletter. The newsletter sends regrade events only — no digests, no
        news feed.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Who reviews, and corrections</h2>
      <p>
        Each compound page names its reviewer and the date of last review. Records marked
        <i>"draft — unverified"</i> have not yet been through full editorial review and should
        be read accordingly. If you find an error — a mis-cited study, a wrong number, a grade
        that doesn't survive contact with the literature — write to the address in the footer.
        Corrections are made in the open: material changes appear in the grade timeline, and
        the full edit history of every record is public in the site's source repository.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Funding and conflicts of interest</h2>
      <p>
        Evidence Stack sells nothing, reviews no brands, and carries no affiliate links or
        sponsorships. Product records exist only to compute cost per gram and are facts from
        labels, not endorsements. If that funding model ever changes, it changes on this page
        first.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The data is citable</h2>
      <p>
        Every record on this site is available as JSON: <a href="/api/compounds.json"><code>/api/compounds.json</code></a>
        for the register index, and <code>/api/compounds/&lt;id&gt;.json</code> for a full
        compound record including claims, studies and grade history. No key, no rate limit
        beyond fair use. If you build on it, a link back is appreciated.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What this site is not</h2>
      <p>
        A summary of published research, however careful, is not medical advice. A grade
        measures evidence, not what you should do — that depends on your health, your
        medication, and your priorities, none of which this site knows.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  article { max-width: 720px; padding: 32px 0 48px; }
  .page-head h1 { font-size: 28px; font-weight: 800; margin: 0 0 12px; }
  .lede { font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.55; color: var(--body); text-wrap: pretty; }
  section { margin-top: 32px; }
  h2 { font-size: 18px; font-weight: 700; margin: 0 0 10px; }
  p { font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--body); margin: 0 0 10px; }
  .grade-list { margin: 14px 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 10px; }
  .grade-row { display: flex; gap: 12px; align-items: flex-start; }
  .grade-text { font-size: 13px; line-height: 1.5; color: var(--body); padding-top: 4px; }
  dl { margin: 0; }
  dt { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 12px; color: var(--amber); margin-top: 10px; }
  dd { margin: 2px 0 0; font-size: 13px; line-height: 1.5; color: var(--body); }
  code { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 12px; }
</style>
